Notebook[{
Cell["Tropical Algebra of ReLU Networks", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"648271d1-e955-4d7f-b738-f91d47c40e0c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A ",
 StyleBox["ReLU neural network is literally a tropical rational map",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". This post takes that statement seriously: we train a tiny 2D classifier \
",
 "in Wolfram Language, rewrite its forward pass using nothing but ",
 StyleBox["max",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["+",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", and show that its decision boundary is a ",
 StyleBox["tropical hypersurface",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " - a finite, piecewise-linear arrangement whose pieces we make explicit. ",
 "We also reproduce the n^2 linear-region scaling predicted by ",
 "Zhang, Naitzat, and Lim (ICML 2018) and by Zaslavsky's classical bound ",
 "on hyperplane arrangements."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"86000f58-5741-4753-919d-fb9507829424"],

Cell["1.  Why ReLU is tropical", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"af304808-f41b-4411-8544-16227b934cdb"],

Cell["\<\
The tropical semiring is the set of real numbers (plus -infinity) with two \
operations:

    a (+) b := max(a, b)        (tropical addition)
    a (x) b := a + b            (tropical multiplication)

The neutral element for (+) is -infinity, and the neutral element for (x) is \
0. The ReLU activation is then simply

    ReLU(x) = max(0, x) = 0 (+) x,

i.e. the tropical addition with the (+) neutral element 0 swapped in for the \
(x) neutral element. An affine map followed by ReLU,

    h(x) = max(0, a . x + b),

is a degree-1 tropical polynomial in (x_1, x_2). A 1-hidden-layer ReLU \
classifier

    f(x) = sum_j c_j max(0, a_j . x + b_j) + d

splits along c_j > 0 vs c_j < 0 into P(x) - Q(x), with both P and Q convex \
piecewise-linear (i.e. tropical polynomials). f is therefore a tropical \
rational map, and the decision boundary {f = 0} is the tropical hypersurface \
{P = Q}.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7258f6f8-6f44-4b2a-800a-a75b79b23642"],

Cell["2.  Train a tiny ReLU classifier", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9fe57943-5279-47f8-b260-9fe3db64cb07"],

Cell["We use two moons as the dataset and a 2 -> 12 -> 1 ReLU MLP.", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9bd513b9-14d3-442e-90a4-f2d442c9a06a"],

Cell["\<\
SeedRandom[7];
makeMoons[nPerClass_: 200, noise_: 0.15] :=
  Module[{th},
    th = Subdivide[0, Pi, nPerClass - 1];
    Join[
      Thread[{Transpose[{Cos[th], Sin[th]}] +
              RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[0, noise], {nPerClass, 2}], 1}],
      Thread[{Transpose[{1 - Cos[th], -Sin[th] - 0.5}] +
              RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[0, noise], {nPerClass, 2}], \
-1}]]
  ];
data = makeMoons[200];
trainData = (#[[1]] -> {N[#[[2]]]}) & /@ data;

netSpec = NetChain[
  {LinearLayer[12], ElementwiseLayer[\"ReLU\"], LinearLayer[1]},
  \"Input\" -> 2];

trained = NetTrain[netSpec, trainData,
  MaxTrainingRounds -> 800, BatchSize -> 64, LearningRate -> 0.01,
  TrainingProgressReporting -> None, RandomSeeding -> 1];

A = Normal @ NetExtract[trained, {1, \"Weights\"}];
b = Normal @ NetExtract[trained, {1, \"Biases\"}];
c = Flatten @ Normal @ NetExtract[trained, {3, \"Weights\"}];
d = First @ Flatten @ Normal @ NetExtract[trained, {3, \"Biases\"}];
{Dimensions[A], Dimensions[b], Dimensions[c]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"257c7468-d3d3-4f80-9de2-400241a2a590"],

Cell["3.  The two forward passes", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"97f77c74-1b6d-4cbe-870f-cd063917f909"],

Cell["\<\
The classical (ReLU, real arithmetic) forward pass is direct. The max-plus \
forward pass pulls every positive output weight inside a max, splits negative \
weights into Q, and never uses subtraction until the final P - Q step:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0e108648-b774-4445-b06d-0bbfc7bce682"],

Cell["\<\
fReLU[x_]    := c . Map[Max[0, #] &, A . x + b] + d;
fMaxPlus[x_] := Module[{P, Q},
  P = Sum[If[c[[j]] > 0,
              Max[0, c[[j]] (A[[j]] . x) + c[[j]] b[[j]]], 0],
           {j, Length[c]}] + Max[d, 0];
  Q = Sum[If[c[[j]] < 0,
              Max[0, -c[[j]] (A[[j]] . x) - c[[j]] b[[j]]], 0],
           {j, Length[c]}] + Max[-d, 0];
  P - Q
];

SeedRandom[123];
xs   = RandomReal[{-2, 2}, {1000, 2}];
yR   = fReLU /@ xs;
yMP  = fMaxPlus /@ xs;
Max[Abs[yR - yMP]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"472f3dac-3daa-413c-b633-483300d60fcb"],

Cell["\<\
The two evaluators agree at machine precision (~10^-15). They are the same \
function, written in two different algebras.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a761b860-dc33-444b-9309-d748fd1de958"],

Cell["4.  Forward-pass equivalence on the three datasets", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"edad944a-ecf0-4293-8094-83780f434255"],

